rophysiology</source><year>1994</year><volume>91</volume><fpage>256</fpage><lpage>264</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Leonard</surname><given-names>CM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Anatomical risk factors that distinguish dyslexia from SLI predict reading skill in normal children</article-title><source>Journal of Communication Disorders</source><year>2002</year><volume>35</volume><fpage>501</fpage><lpage>531</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12443050</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lincoln</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Sensory modulation of auditory stimuli in children with autism and receptive developmental language disorder &#x02013; event related potential evidence</article-title><source>Journal of Autism and Developmental Disorders</source><year>1995</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>521</fpage><lpage>539</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8567597</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>McArthur</surname><given-names>GM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Which people with specific language impairment have auditory processing deficits?</article-title><source>Cognitive Neuropsychology</source><year>2004</year><volume>21</volume><fpage>79</fpage><lpage>94</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Marler</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Auditory memory for backward masking signals in children with language impairment</article-title><source>Psychophysiology</source><year>2002</year><volume>39</volume><fpage>767</fpage><lpage>780</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12462505</pub-id></citation